pfister_@mindspring.com (Jim Crawford) writes:
> I'm playing an extremely lucky Tourist on on the alt.org nethack
> servers.  I just ran across a bones file on level *26*, just before
> the castle.  Headstone says "Congressman, killed by an invisible
> arch-lich."  Good thing I genocided those, huh?

> I guess I have two questions.  Firstly, it was a maze level, are there
> usually maze levels between medusa and the castle?

Yes. (If there are any such levels at all, they have a 4/5 chance of
being mazes.)

>  It doesn't appear
> to be Gehennom, but that would be my second guess, that it's from a
> game in which level 26 happened to be Gehennom.

Gehennom bones are kept separate from Dungeon bones; you can't get
branch mixing in this manner.
 
> Secondly, is there anything preventing early dungeon level, late-game
> bones file?  Seems like it'd be a bit extreme in either the lucky find
> or the annoying death direction.  I suppose just the unlikelihood of
> dying at that point in the game may be preventative enough.

Nothing stopping it except that, as you note, most characters just zip
through the top few levels on their way to the DL1 upstair. A bit of
bad timing with a nasties-summon or a worse than usual run-in with the
Wizard is about the only thing that's particularly likely to lead to
this; the chances of this happening, the chances of leaving bones at
low levels, and the relatively low number of characters reaching the
return-with-the-Amulet point in the first place, mean that it's not a
huge problem.

(Unfortunately, there's no easy way to tell automatically whether a
given bones level is a result of this, since the most tell-tale signs
are converted to innocuous objects before the bones are
saved. Otherwise Alexis might have been able to tell us how many such
lurk in Hearse.)
 
